# DORC Workspace Rules

This workspace contains multiple repositories that together implement **DORC**  
(Deterministic Orchestration of Rectified Canon).

These rules are authoritative and must be followed by all code generated or modified in this workspace.

---

## Architecture Principles

- BigQuery is the **ledger of validity**.
  - All validation results must be written to BigQuery.
  - BigQuery rows are the **evidence of record**, not LLM output or logs.

- Validation is **one-time per run**.
  - Never re-validate the same chunk for the same run.
  - Never recompute validation during promotion.

- Promotion **replays approved state** only.
  - Promotion must read from BigQuery.
  - Promotion must NOT re-parse files.
  - Promotion must NOT call Gemini or any LLM.

- Fail fast. No silent success.
  - Any parsing error, missing data, API failure, or LLM failure must surface as an explicit failure.
  - Never swallow errors or downgrade failures to “PASS by default”.

- Determinism over convenience.
  - Deterministic chunking, deterministic IDs, reproducible runs.
  - LLMs may assist judgment, but must return structured, machine-readable output.

- No repo may depend on another repo’s source code.
  - All cross-repo coordination happens via BigQuery and explicit APIs only.


---

## Cloud-Only Deployment & Logging

**CRITICAL: This entire project runs exclusively in Google Cloud. There is NO local testing or local development environment.**

- **ALL services run in Google Cloud** (Cloud Run, Firebase Hosting, etc.)
- **NEVER assume localhost or local development** - everything is deployed to cloud
- **ALL debugging, logging, and instrumentation MUST use cloud-compatible methods**

### Logging Requirements

- **For JavaScript/TypeScript (Next.js, React, etc.):**
  - Use console.log(), console.error(), console.warn() for all debugging output
  - These logs are automatically captured by Google Cloud Logging / Firebase Logging
  - Format logs as: console.log('[DEBUG]', JSON.stringify({...})) for structured logging
  - **NEVER use localhost endpoints** (e.g., http://127.0.0.1:7242/...) - these will NOT work in cloud
  - **NEVER use fetch() to localhost** for logging - use console methods only

- **For Python services:**
  - Use Google Cloud Logging client library: rom google.cloud import logging
  - Or use standard Python logging module - logs are automatically captured by Cloud Run
  - **NEVER write to local files** for logging - use cloud logging APIs

- **For all languages:**
  - All logs must be accessible via Google Cloud Console Logs Explorer
  - Search for [DEBUG] prefix to find instrumentation logs
  - Logs are collected automatically by the cloud platform - no manual log file collection needed

### Debugging & Instrumentation

- When adding debugging instrumentation:
  1. Use cloud-compatible logging methods (console.log, Google Cloud Logging, etc.)
  2. Structure logs with clear prefixes (e.g., [DEBUG], [ERROR], [INFO])
  3. Include structured data in JSON format for easy querying
  4. **NEVER** attempt to send logs to localhost endpoints
  5. **NEVER** create local log files that need manual collection

- To view logs:
  - Use Google Cloud Console → Logs Explorer
  - Use Firebase Console → Functions/Logs (for Firebase-hosted services)
  - Search by log prefix, timestamp, or structured data fields

### Testing & Development

- All testing happens in cloud environments (staging/production)
- There is no local development server or local testing environment
- All changes must be deployed to cloud to be tested
- Never assume code will run locally - it only runs in cloud
---

## Repository Boundaries

Each repository has a strict responsibility boundary:

- `dorc-infra`
  - Infrastructure only.
  - BigQuery schemas, IAM requirements, Cloud Build trigger specs.
  - No application logic.

- `dorc-engine`
  - Runs on Pull Requests only.
  - Parses canon markdown into atomic chunks.
  - Calls Gemini for validation.
  - Writes ALL results to BigQuery.
  - Posts a report link back to the PR.
  - Never promotes data to production.

- `dorc-promoter`
  - Runs on merge to main only.
  - Reads approved chunks from BigQuery.
  - Generates Atomic JSONL.
  - Imports into Vertex AI RAG.
  - Never re-parses files.
  - Never calls Gemini or any LLM.

- `dorc-rectifier`
  - Phase 2 system.
  - Reads failed validation runs from BigQuery.
  - Generates proposed canon fixes (copy/paste safe by default).
  - Never auto-merges or overwrites canon.

- `dorc-reports`
  - Documentation and human inspection only.
  - Looker Studio report IDs, URLs, and filter contracts.
  - No runtime or pipeline dependencies.

---

## LLM Usage Rules

- LLMs are allowed ONLY where explicitly intended (e.g., `dorc-engine`, `dorc-rectifier`).
- LLMs must return **strict structured output** (e.g., JSON).
- No free-form prose as control signals.
- LLM output must always be persisted to BigQuery when it influences system state.

---

## Code Style & Quality

- Use Python type hints.
- Keep modules small and single-purpose.
- Write tests for deterministic components (chunking, ID generation, schema mapping).
- Document contracts and interfaces explicitly (especially BigQuery row contracts).

---

## Cleanup & Artifact Management

- **ALWAYS clean up compiled Python files (`.pyc` files) after completing any task.**
  - Any file that ends in `.pyc` must be deleted immediately after it is created or after the task is complete.
  - Delete all `.pyc` files and `__pycache__/` directories before completing any task.
  - This is a **mandatory cleanup step** that must be performed at the end of every task.
  
- **Always clean up compiled artifacts and temporary files** after running code or tests.
  - Delete all `.pyc` files and `__pycache__/` directories before committing.
  - Remove any temporary test files, log files, or build artifacts created during development.
  - Never commit compiled Python bytecode (`.pyc` files) or cache directories.
  - If you run code that generates artifacts, clean them up immediately after.
  
- **Before completing any task**, verify no compiled artifacts or temporary files remain in the repository.
  - Run cleanup commands to remove all `.pyc` files: `find . -name "*.pyc" -delete` or equivalent.
  - Remove `__pycache__/` directories: `find . -type d -name "__pycache__" -exec rm -r {} +` or equivalent.

---

## Speech-to-Text Normalization

- In this workspace context:
  - “DORK” must always be interpreted as **DORC**.
  - Repo names must use dash-separated form (e.g., `dorc-engine`).

---

## When Making Changes

- Always verify which repository you are working in.
- Respect repository boundaries — do not introduce cross-repo source dependencies.
- Any BigQuery schema change must be made in `dorc-infra/bq/` first.
- Follow the flow:
  **validation → promotion → rectification**
- Human-facing output should prefer links and reports over inline text dumps.

---

These rules exist to preserve determinism, auditability, and long-term correctness.

